otal does not reconcile.\n\n4. EXPENDITURE INTERNAL INCONSISTENCY: The report states current monthly expenditure of £2,986 in the fact-find narrative but uses £2,237/month in the cashflow model — a £749/month difference with no reconciliation. This is a self-contradiction in the report (flagged in the extraction's internalInconsistencies). One candidate explanation is that the £2,237 figure nets out Tony's £600 contribution and/or the mortgage, but even that does not reconcile arithmetically: £2,986 − £600 (Tony) − £750 (mortgage) = £1,636, not £2,237; or £2,986 − £749 = £2,237 exactly, suggesting the mortgage (£750≈£749 rounding) may have been excluded but Tony's contribution was not netted. No explanation is given in the report.\n\n5. STATE PENSION £230.21/WEEK: This figure appears only in the report and is not corroborated by any evidence document. Absence of corroboration is a finding under the check's stated scope.\n\n6. SAVINGS CONFLICT BETWEEN EVIDENCE DOCUMENTS: The meeting summary [P13] records ~£17,000 in savings while the fact find [P8] records £7,000. This is a conflict within the evidence itself; the report does not explicitly state a savings balance in the financial position section so it cannot be tested directly, but the supporting documents disagree — this is a data quality issue in the case file.\n\nThe mortgage and Tony's contribution discrepancies affect the income/expenditure picture used to support the recommendation context. The total assets figure is materially wrong or unexplained. The cashflow modelling uses an expenditure figure £749/month lower than the stated total without explanation. These are material inconsistencies.","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Melanie Anness","Other Income","Partner's Contributio","Monthly","£ 0.00","£ 800.00"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Fact Find (1).md","category":"B","quote":"She has stopped working and therefore lost employment income, she h