 her current position.","Cashflow model does not separately show mortgage of £600/month alongside the £2,237 expenditure figure; the report lists both mortgage (£750/month for 7 years) and expenditure (£2,237) as separate cashflow line items, but the relationship between the £2,237 figure and the fact-find total of £2,986 (which already includes the £600 mortgage) is unexplained.","No cashflow modelling output document is evidenced in the file; the cashflow conclusions are supported only by the suitability report itself (all cashflow passages are [I])."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Independent cashflow modelling output or tool report to corroborate the modelled inputs and scenario outcomes","Reconciliation of the £2,237 cashflow expenditure figure to the £2,986 fact-find figure (whether the £600 mortgage and/or Tony's contribution net down to £2,237 or whether £2,237 is an error)","Confirmation of current employment/income status following the July 2026 profile note that Melanie has stopped working","Up-to-date confirmation of the inheritance amount and expected receipt date"],"analysis":"Multiple material discrepancies exist between the cashflow model inputs used in the report and the figures recorded in the supporting fact find.\n\n1. EXPENDITURE DISCREPANCY (£749/month): The report states total monthly expenditure as £2,986 in the 'Your current situation' section but uses £2,237/month in the cashflow model. No reconciliation is provided. One candidate reading is that £2,237 = £2,986 − £600 (mortgage) − £149 (unknown), or that it nets off Tony's contribution; another is that it is simply an error. The extraction flags this as an unresolved ambiguity. Either way, the cashflow model uses a materially lower expenditure figure than the fact find without explanation, which means the cashflow's surplus income and hence the no-shortfall conclusion may be overstated.\n\n2. MORTGAGE INPUT: The cashflow model uses £750/month for 7 years. The fact find [P17]