ich remains a discrepancy.\n\n6. NO CASHFLOW MODELLING OUTPUT ON FILE: All cashflow passages are sourced from [I] (the suitability report itself). There is no independent cashflow tool output, schedule or annex in the evidence pack. The cashflow conclusions — including the baseline no-shortfall result and the stress tests — are therefore unsubstantiated by any document other than the report's own narrative. The report cannot corroborate itself.\n\n7. VULNERABILITY OVERLAY: The profile note [P14] records that Melanie has type 2 diabetes and that as at July 2026 she has stopped working, which reduces her financial resilience. The cashflow's income assumption (£45,000 until 60) does not reflect this. Where a client has potential health-related vulnerability and a recent material change in income, the accuracy of cashflow inputs is heightened in importance from a suitability perspective.\n\nThe guards do not resolve these issues. The mortgage amount difference (£750 vs £600) is not a growth-assumption difference. The inheritance amount difference (£350,000 vs £150,000) is not a chart-parsing ambiguity. The expenditure discrepancy is an identified internal inconsistency in the report itself. The income staling is a file-level finding, not a rounding difference. Multiple inputs that drive the affordability conclusion are inconsistent with the fact find, and no independent cashflow output is on file to validate them.","citations":[{"passageId":"P13","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"Your monthly expenditure totals £2,986 and Tony contributes £600 per month towards the household bills.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P24","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"Your expenditure is £2,237 per month until retirement.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P24","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"You pay £750 per month on your mortgage for the next 7 years.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Fact Find (1).