arge, making the like-for-like basis unclear — the report excludes ongoing advice but includes it in the 0.98% figure creating an inconsistent comparison basis.","The ongoing service charge monetary equivalent of £3,181.08 p.a. is broadly reconcilable only if the PSO funds of ~£260,000 are included in the base, but this combined total is never explicitly stated as the basis, leaving the figure without a transparent derivation in the report.","The initial advice fee of £9,838.36 stated as 3% of amount invested cannot be reconciled with 3% of the stated transfer value of £67,945.44 (which gives £2,038.36). The basis including PSO funds (~£327,945) is never explicitly stated.","No switch analysis covering the Quilter PSO is present in the case file, so the cost-benefit justification for that transfer (£208 saving, 0.19% vs 0.27%) rests solely on the report's own assertion with no independent corroboration."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Switch charges analysis or provider documentation for the Quilter Pension Sharing Order transfer (to corroborate the 0.27% Quilter charge and £208 annual saving)","Basis for the critical yield of 1.23% for Nest — the implied fund value of £1,556.91 does not match any stated figure","Explicit statement of the combined fund value (£327,945 including PSO) used as the basis for the initial fee of £9,838.36 and ongoing charge of £3,181.08"],"analysis":"The report makes several cost-benefit claims that require scrutiny under G7.10. Three issues stand out as material findings after applying the guards.\n\n1. AVIVA PLATFORM CHARGE INCONSISTENCY: The report uses 0.21% as the Aviva platform charge when comparing against True Potential, but 0.19% when comparing against Quilter and as the negotiated rate in the charges table. The derived figures confirm 0.19% is applied to the full combined fund. This is an internal inconsistency within the report — two different rates for the same Aviva platform charge in the same recommendation document — which is not explained by any guard (the guard about different bases applies where two documents use different bases, but here the report itself uses two different figures). The 0.21% vs 0.40% comparison for True Potential and the resulting £88.68 saving may therefore be overstated if the true rate is 0.19%.\n\n2. QUILTER SWITCH — NO CORROBORATING EVIDENCE: The claim that Aviva is cheaper than Quilter by £208 p.a. (0.19% vs 0.27%) is made only in the report [P24, I category]. No switch analysis, Quilter provider document, or other non-report source in the case file corroborates the 0.27% Quilter charge or the £208 saving. Per the instruction, a claim supported only by [I] passages has no support in the case file. This is the most significant evidence gap given that the Quilter PSO transfer involves approximately £260,000 — the largest single transfer. The benefits listed for the Quilter transfer (lower cost, monitoring, flexibility) are stated but unverified against any independent document in the pack.\n\n3. CRITICAL YIELD FOR NEST (1.23%) — ARITHMETIC INCONSISTENCY: The derived figures show that 1.23% applied to the required outperformance of £19.15 p.a. implies a fund of only £1,556.91 — which matches no arrangement. The Nest fund value is £21,272.64. If the critical yield were applied to £21,272.64, 1.23% would imply an annual shortfall of ~£261.65, not £19.15. The £19.15 figure is the annual cost difference at 0.09% of £21,272.64 (arithmetically consistent). But the critical yield of 1.23% applied to this base produces ~£261.65 annual required outperformance, not £19.15. The 1.23% figure therefore appears to have been computed on a different (much smaller) base, and is not reconcilable with the Nest fund value. This is a data inconsistency that could mislead the client about the hurdle rate they face.\n\n4. TRUE POTENTIAL LIKE-FOR-LIKE BASIS: The 0.98% existing charge for True Potential includes 0.50% ongoing advice charge per the internalInconsistencies note. Excluding advice from both sides to get like-for-like: existing = 0.48%, new = 0.39%, difference = 0.09% — not 0.59%. The 0.59% saving uses existing 0.98% vs new 0.39% — i.e. it excludes advice on the new side but includes it on the existing side. The report acknowledges it excludes ongoing advice, but the 0.98% figure it uses apparently includes it. This is an inconsistent comparison basis that inflates the stated saving from 0.09% to 0.59%.\n\nVulnerability overlay: No specific vulnerability characteristics are identified in the evidence that would materially change this assessment, though the arithmetic inconsistencies noted above are particularly concerning where clients may be relying on clear cost disclosure to make an informed decision.\n\nThe guards considered: consolidation convenience vs financial benefit — the report does not rely solely on consolidation; it relies on specific charge percentages and monetary savings which are the subject of the discrepancies. Benefits the existing plans already provide — flexible drawdown and online access are generic to both platforms, but the report's primary justification rests on cost savings, not these ancillary benefits. The charge basis guard — the True Potential 0.98% vs 0.39% comparison uses different bases (one including advice charge, one excluding) making the comparison invalid on its own terms.","citations":[{"passageId":"P24","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"We have negotiated a reduced platform charges of 0.19%, compared to the Quilter charges of 0.27%, representing a savings of £208 per annum, based on the estimated fund value.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P24","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"The platform charges with Aviva are 0.21% compared to the True Potential platform charges of 0.40%, this represents a saving of 0.19% or £88.68 based on the current value of the fund.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Annes M SAP IAC only ex 