e of the fee and its impact on the invested amount are not transparently disclosed.","The SAP documents [P3, P14] reference a Lifetime Limit / A-Day allowance figure of £1.0731m (2021/22 basis, noting CPI indexation from April 2026), whereas the report quotes the Lump Sum Allowance as £268,275. These are different allowances (lifetime allowance legacy vs. post-2024 lump sum allowance regime) but the report does not reconcile or clarify the distinction for the client, which could cause confusion about what limits apply.","The report states the IHT consequence in generic legislative terms for the 2027 change without quantifying the likely IHT liability after receipt of the inheritance, even though the report itself acknowledges the inheritance will take the client over the threshold."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Client's marginal income tax rate or tax band (needed to make the drawdown tax consequence client-specific)","Projected retirement fund value (needed to contextualise income tax on future crystallisation)"],"analysis":"The report provides a broad and reasonably comprehensive tax disclosure section covering the main heads: contribution relief, TFLS, drawdown income tax, death benefits, annual allowance, MPAA, IHT and the 2027 legislative change, and a tax-law caveat. These are corroborated by the Aviva KFD [P5], T&C [P2] and illustration [P7]. Against the 'generic legislative explanation is not the same as stating the consequence for this client' guard: the drawdown income tax point is stated purely generically — the report never establishes the client's marginal income tax rate. The IHT section personalises the position (assets currently below £500,000 threshold, inheritance will take her over) but does not quantify the projected liability, which would have been possible given the facts in the report. These are at most moderate concerns: the generic income tax point is standard industry disclosure and the rate guard means it is unverifiable rather than wrong, but the absence of any personalisation of the drawdown tax charge for a client specifically planning to access drawdown falls short of the 'client-specific' standard. More materially, the switch analysis documents [P3, P4] show the proposed Aviva plan will provide only 97% of the existing death benefit (£65,907 vs £67,945) immediately post-switch. The report does not mention this reduction anywhere, nor its tax implications. This is a material disadvantage identified in the supporting evidence and absent from the report. The A-Day/lifetime allowance figure of £1.0731m in the SAP documents refers to the pre-2024 lifetime allowance regime; the report correctly cites the current £268,275 Lump Sum Allowance. These are different allowances and the discrepancy reflects legacy document wording in the SAP rather than an error in the report — this difference is explained by the fact the two passages address different regulatory concepts. The irreconcilable initial fee figure (£9,838.36 vs 3% of stated transfer value) is primarily a CHK-007 matter, but to the extent it affects tax transparency (the fee is deducted before investment, reducing the tax-relieved amount), the report's failure to state the basis for the charge is a disclosure gap here too. On vulnerability: no specific vulnerability is flagged in the file that would require enhanced tax explanation, and CHK-010 covers that assessment. Overall: the death-benefit reduction disclosed in the switch analysis but absent from the report is the principal concern under this requirement.","citations":[{"passageId":"P5","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"You can normally withdraw up to 25% of your pension fund as a tax-free lump sum. You may have to pay income tax on any income payments and lump sum payments you take. Both are treated as income, and therefore the tax you pay will depend on your personal circumstances.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Annes M SAP All charges inc