otiated reduction of 0.08%, giving 0.17%, plus fund charges (HSBC 0.21%, Vanguard 0.2207%, weighted avg 0.2154%). The Quilter charge comparison (0.19% vs 0.27%) and the fund-range claim (6,900+ vs 6) are asserted in the report only — no independent provider documentation confirms the Quilter platform charge of 0.27% or the Quilter fund count of 6. The SAP tools show the Aviva plan is cheaper on a like-for-like basis excluding existing adviser charges (True Potential), but more expensive when all charges including the new adviser charge are included (Nest). No passage in categories E or G independently evidences the Quilter charge of 0.27%, the fund count comparison, or that no safeguarded benefits exist in the Quilter PSO.","discrepancies":["1. The report claims the Aviva platform charge is 0.19%; P4 states the standard AMC is 0.25% reduced by 0.08%, giving 0.17% — a 0.02% discrepancy vs the 0.19% stated in the report's own charges table (P13). These may be reconcilable: 0.25% − 0.08% = 0.17% for investment management plus a separate platform fee, but the arithmetic does not straightforwardly produce 0.19%.","2. The Quilter platform charge of 0.27% appears only in the suitability report (P16, category I). No independent provider document or category E/G source confirms this figure.","3. The fund range comparison of 6,900+ (Aviva) vs 6 (Quilter) appears only in the report (P15, category I). No external source confirms the Quilter fund count of 6.","4. P2 (SAP all charges including existing OAC) shows Aviva PCLS of £26,600 vs existing schemes £30,400 at retirement, meaning on a total-charges-included basis the switch leaves the client with less at retirement. The report does not address this scenario from the SAP.","5. No passage in categories E or G confirms the absence of safeguarded benefits or guarantees in the Quilter PSO, despite P9 (Aviva KFD) explicitly warning that transferring from an older company pension could involve losing guaranteed income or valuable rights. The report asserts no safeguarded benefits are lost but this claim is unsupported by any evidence document.","6. The 'no exit penalty' assertion for all three ceding plans is stated only in the report (category I passages); no provider exit confirmation letter or category E document is on file to corroborate it for the Quilter PSO in particular.","7. The report states the Nest switch requires 1.23% pa outperformance to match projected values. The SAP (P1, all charges inc OAC) confirms this scenario is modelled, but the Nest switch remains loss-making on a total-charge basis with no benefit other than qualitative ones (adviser servicing, fund range, flexi-access). The file contains no evidence that Nest does not already offer flexi-access drawdown or that its fund range is limited to the extent claimed.","8. The internal inconsistency noted in the extraction — True Potential platform charge stated as 0.40% in existing arrangements table but switch charges table uses 0.98% including existing ongoing advice charge of 0.50% — means the like-for-like saving of 0.59% (which excludes the 0.50% existing OAC) is presented as a cost benefit of switching, when in practice the client will be paying 1% ongoing advice going forward vs 0.50% previously. The net position on adviser charges is a cost increase of 0.50% pa, which the report does not highlight as a cost of switching."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Independent evidence (category E or G) of the Quilter platform charge of 0.27%","Independent evidence (category E or G) of the Quilter fund range count (claimed as 6)","Provider confirmation or scheme documentation confirming the Quilter PSO contains no safeguarded benefits, guaranteed annuity rates or other valuable features","Provider confirmation of nil exit charges from Quilter PSO (no category E exit confirmation letter on file)","Confirmation of whether Nest offers flexi-access drawdown, to validate the claim this is a benefit gained by switching"],"analysis":"The requirement is to assess whether evidence supports claims that higher costs are justified by additional benefits. This requires looking at each limb.\n\nFor the True Potential switch: The SAP documents (P3, P5) corroborate a genuine like-for-like charge saving of 0.59% pa excluding the existing ongoing advice charge. However, the existing OAC of 0.50% is being replaced by a new OAC of 1.00% — a net increase of 0.50% pa in adviser charges. The SAP (P1, including existing OAC) shows Aviva projected fund of £73,156 vs True Potential £73,156 at 2.94% (paid up) — the report's own 'can underperform by 0.01%' figure is very thin. The file contains evidence supporting the charges comparison for True Potential, though the net adviser charge increase is not disclosed as a cost.\n\nFor the Nest switch: The report openly acknowledges the new plan is 0.09% / £19.15 pa more expensive on a like-for-like basis and requires 1.23% pa outperformance including adviser charges. The SAP documents corroborate the charge modelling. However, the benefits claimed to justify this cost increase — wider fund range, flexi-access drawdown, adviser servicing — are stated only in the report (category I) with no independent evidence. There is no evidence that Nest lacks flexi-access drawdown or has a fund range so limited that this constitutes a genuine additional benefit. P9 (Aviva KFD) confirms Aviva offers these features but does not evidence that Nest lacks them.\n\nFor the Quilter PSO switch: The report claims a cost saving (0.19% vs 0.27%) but neither the Quilter charge of 0.27% nor the fund count of 6 is evidenced by any category E or G document. More seriously, the Quilter PSO arises from a pension sharing order against an ex-husband's pension. P9 (Aviva KFD) explicitly warns that defined benefit or older company pension schemes may carry guaranteed income or valuable rights that would be lost. No document in the file confirms that the Quilter PSO contains no safeguarded benefits, guaranteed annuity rates or other valuable features. The report's assertion that no safeguarded benefits are lost is entirely unsupported by evidence. This is the highest severity concern per the severity guidance.\n\nOn the fund range comparison guard: the report claims 6,900+ vs 6 funds. P15 confirms Aviva has 6,900+ but this is a report passage (category I). No independent source confirms the Quilter fund count. This does not count as corroboration.\n\nThe internal inconsistency regarding the True Potential charge basis (0.40% platform in existing table vs 0.98% in switch table including 0.50% OAC) means the like-for-like saving of 0.59% is presented in a way that does not make clear to the reader that the ongoing adviser charge is doubling from 0.50% to 1.00%, which is a disclosure shortfall.\n\nOverall: the Quilter safeguarded benefits gap and absence of corroboration for the key cost comparison figures are material concerns. The Nest cost increase is acknowledged but justified only by benefits that are not evidenced from external sources. No vulnerability-related adjustment is required: the file notes the client is recently divorced and self-employed but no specific vulnerability drivers are recorded that would alter the weight of this analysis.","citations":[{"passageId":"P16","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"We have negotiated a reduced platform charges of 0.19%, compared to the Quilter charges of 0.27%, representing a savings of £208 per annum, based on the estimated fund value.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"the new scheme offers access to a wider range of investment options, allowing for a more tailored investment strategy that better suits your risk profile and retirement goals, and allowing greater choice should your risk profile change in the future.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"our calculations showed that the Aviva plan is more expensive than your current Nest plan. Based on an annual growth rate of 2.94%, this means that your proposed plan will need to outperform your current plan by 1.23% per annum to age 89 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"our calculations showed that the Aviva plan is more expensive than your current True Potential plan. Based on an annual growth rate of 2.94%, this means that your proposed plan can underperform your current plan by 0.01% per annum to age 89 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"If you're transferring benefits from an older company pension scheme it could be a Defined Benefit Pension and you may be losing valuable rights or benefits within the scheme such as a guaranteed income based on your final salary.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P9","source":"Aviva Pension KFD.md","category":"H","quote":"Transferring pensions isn't right for everyone. It could be a complex decision and you need to consider the charges, fund ranges, any valuable benefits that could be lost and tax implications.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Annes M SAP All charges inc existing OAC 24.3.26.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing Schemes","£30,400"]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Annes M SAP All charges inc existing OAC 24.3.26.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva Pension Portfolio (Choice)","£26,600"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Annes M SAP IAC only ex existing OAC 24.3.26.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing Schemes","£33,600"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Annes M SAP IAC only ex existing OAC 24.3.26.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva Pension Portfolio (Choice)","£36,600"]},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Annes M SAP All charges ex existing OAC 24.3.26.md","category":"G","quote":"Annual Management Charge: 0.25% pa","cells":null},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Annes M SAP All charges ex existing OAC 24.3.26.md","category":"G","quote":"Decrease in Annual Management Charge of 0.08%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":null,"cells":["Aviva Platform Personal Pension","0.19%","0.20%","0.39%","£623.10","£655.89","£1,278.99"]}],"issueCategories":["Missing evidence","Weak justification","Disclosure shortfall"],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

