{"groupId":"G10.3","requirement":"Adjustments made to support client comprehension — are the support/communication adjustments asserted by the report backed by evidence in the case file?","reportSays":"The suitability report records two vulnerability-adjacent characteristics: (1) recently divorced with a Pension Sharing Order (significant life event) and (2) recently bereaved (late mother, pending inheritance). The report notes the client has a background in accounting and currency trading and describes her as having a fair degree of knowledge and understanding. The canonical model extraction flags /vulnerability/perClient[]/adjustmentsMade[] as absent from the extracted model, with the extraction's own log recording the path /clientAuthority/vulnerabilityAssessment/detail as NotPresentInDocument. The report therefore asserts two life-event vulnerability drivers but records no explicit vulnerability assessment and no adjustments made to support client comprehension.","fileSays":"The case file (meeting summaries [P8], [P15], [P6]) corroborates the divorce and bereavement circumstances. [P8] notes the client is 'a seasoned professional with a background in accounting and currency trading' and that she 'recognise[s] the value of professional advice'. [P15] records the divorce and pension-sharing order process. [P6] records bereavement context (inheritance from late mother's estate, complicated by legal proceedings). The fact find [B] passages are largely blank on vulnerability, communication preferences and support needs. No passage in any category records a formal vulnerability assessment, any adjustment to communication format, language, pace or medium, any accessible format provision, any third-party or carer involvement, or any specific measure taken to support comprehension in light of the two recorded life events. The meeting summaries record practical administrative steps (letter of authority, paper-based Quilter process) but nothing framed as a vulnerability-driven adjustment.","discrepancies":["The report records two FG21/1 life-event vulnerability drivers (divorce and bereavement) but no vulnerability assessment is present anywhere in the file.","The report is silent on any adjustments made to support client comprehension; no adjustment record exists in the file either.","No communication preference, accessible format or language requirement is recorded in either the report or any supporting document.","No third-party involvement, trusted contact, Power of Attorney or family support arrangement is recorded or ruled out in either the report or the file despite the client being recently bereaved and divorced.","The fact find vulnerability/support-needs fields are blank; no alternative record compensates for this."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"Two clear FG21/1 life-event drivers are present: divorce with a Pension Sharing Order and bereavement. Under FCA FG21/1 these are recognised triggers requiring the adviser to consider whether the client may be temporarily or permanently vulnerable and to record the outcome of that consideration, together with any adjustments made. The report itself acknowledges the circumstances but the extraction confirms that /clientAuthority/vulnerabilityAssessment/detail is NotPresentInDocument — meaning the report contains no formal vulnerability assessment section, not merely that extraction failed to read one. No passage in categories B, C or H records a vulnerability screening outcome, a decision that no adjustment was needed, or any positive step taken to adapt the advice process. The client's accounting background and stated financial literacy are noted and may inform a judgment that no additional support was needed, but that judgment is never made explicit and is not a substitute for a recorded assessment. The absence is therefore a process gap: the drivers were captured, the assessment was not conducted or not recorded, and no adjustments (or reasoned decision that none were needed) appear anywhere in the file. This is a Moderate finding: support needs associated with two recorded life events are not reflected in any communication or adjustment record, which falls short of the Consumer Duty obligations under PRIN 2A.2.1R, PRIN 2A.5 and FCA FG21/1.","citations":[{"passageId":"P8","source":"Meeting Summary with Jack.md","category":"C","quote":"You are a seasoned professional with a background in accounting and currency trading, which provides you with a solid understanding of financial matters.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Meeting Summary with Jack Davidson.md","category":"C","quote":"You are a client of us who is currently in the process of managing your financial affairs following your recent divorce.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Meeting Summary.md","category":"C","quote":"At 55, Melanie is considering accessing her pension benefits to support herself financially during a transitional phase","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Meeting Summary.md","category":"C","quote":"Melanie is anticipating an inheritance of approximately £150,000 from her mother's estate, which is currently complicated by legal challenges involving her mother's ex-partner.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability report 2026.md","category":"I","quote":"You completed a risk profiling questionnaire, and this was discussed and agreed. Your risk profile was agreed as 7 on a scale of 1 - 10.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Missing evidence","Process risk","Disclosure shortfall"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

